1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,028
Respiration is the stepwise oxidation of food to release usable energy.

2
00:00:05,028 --> 00:00:12,400
Burn glucose in a flame and about two thousand eight hundred and seventy kilojoules per mole comes out at once, as heat.

3
00:00:12,633 --> 00:00:18,715
A cell releases it in small steps and traps much of it in ATP, in four stages.

4
00:00:18,715 --> 00:00:19,836
We keep a running tally.

5
00:00:21,066 --> 00:00:24,805
Stage one, glycolysis, in the cytoplasm.

6
00:00:24,805 --> 00:00:28,227
It needs no oxygen and runs in every living cell.

7
00:00:28,433 --> 00:00:31,885
First two ATP are spent adding phosphates.

8
00:00:31,885 --> 00:00:35,170
The step at phosphofructokinase is the rate-limiting one.

9
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:38,956
The sugar splits into two three-carbon pieces.

10
00:00:38,956 --> 00:00:44,755
Each makes one NADH and two ATP directly, by substrate-level phosphorylation.

11
00:00:44,966 --> 00:00:47,064
Result: two pyruvate.

12
00:00:47,064 --> 00:00:53,637
Gross four ATP, minus two invested: net two ATP and two NADH.

13
00:00:54,866 --> 00:00:58,890
Stage two, the link reaction, in the mitochondrial matrix.

14
00:00:59,100 --> 00:01:10,243
Each pyruvate loses a carbon as carbon dioxide and makes NADH, leaving acetyl coenzyme A. Per glucose: two carbon dioxide, two NADH.

15
00:01:11,466 --> 00:01:15,231
Stage three, the Krebs cycle, in the matrix.

16
00:01:15,231 --> 00:01:22,162
Acetyl coenzyme A, two carbons, joins oxaloacetate, four, to make citrate, six.

17
00:01:22,366 --> 00:01:29,907
Isocitrate loses a carbon dioxide and makes NADH, giving alpha-ketoglutarate, five carbons.

18
00:01:29,907 --> 00:01:36,147
A second carbon dioxide and NADH give succinyl coenzyme A, four carbons.

19
00:01:36,366 --> 00:01:45,734
Then one ATP, one FADH two at the succinate step, and a third NADH as malate returns to oxaloacetate.

20
00:01:45,966 --> 00:01:53,858
Per turn: three NADH, one FADH two, one ATP, two carbon dioxide.

21
00:01:53,858 --> 00:01:55,543
It turns twice per glucose.

22
00:01:55,766 --> 00:01:59,882
Tally: six carbon dioxide, so every carbon is gone.

23
00:01:59,882 --> 00:02:06,904
Ten NADH, two FADH two, only four ATP, and no oxygen used yet.

24
00:02:09,500 --> 00:02:14,957
Stage four, the electron transport system, on the inner mitochondrial membrane.

25
00:02:14,957 --> 00:02:27,380
Electrons from NADH pass through complex one, ubiquinone, complex three, cytochrome c and complex four to oxygen, the final acceptor, forming water.

26
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:35,336
Complexes one, three and four pump four, four and two protons: ten per NADH.

27
00:02:35,336 --> 00:02:41,142
FADH two enters at complex two, which pumps none, so it gives six.

28
00:02:41,366 --> 00:02:46,433
About four protons through ATP synthase make one ATP.

29
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:53,438
So NADH is worth two point five ATP, FADH two one point five.

30
00:02:53,438 --> 00:02:55,527
This is Mitchell's chemiosmosis.

31
00:02:57,200 --> 00:03:03,855
Add it up: two plus two, plus twenty-five from NADH, plus three from FADH two.

32
00:03:03,855 --> 00:03:04,516
Thirty-two.

33
00:03:04,733 --> 00:03:09,656
But the two glycolysis NADH must be shuttled into the mitochondrion.

34
00:03:09,656 --> 00:03:15,490
The malate aspartate shuttle keeps them at two point five each: thirty-two.

35
00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:20,672
The glycerol phosphate shuttle drops them to one point five: thirty.

36
00:03:20,672 --> 00:03:23,431
So, thirty to thirty-two ATP.

37
00:03:25,233 --> 00:03:26,602
Exam tip.

38
00:03:26,602 --> 00:03:34,584
Older books give thirty-eight, assuming exactly three ATP per NADH and two per FADH two.

39
00:03:34,584 --> 00:03:37,683
Chemiosmosis needs no whole numbers.

40
00:03:37,683 --> 00:03:41,860
Write thirty to thirty-two, and call thirty-eight the classical figure.

41
00:03:42,633 --> 00:03:47,465
Without oxygen, the chain stops and NAD plus runs out.

42
00:03:47,465 --> 00:03:53,911
Fermentation turns NADH back into NAD plus so glycolysis can continue.

43
00:03:54,133 --> 00:03:58,705
Yeast and waterlogged roots make ethanol and carbon dioxide.

44
00:03:58,705 --> 00:04:04,221
Muscle and Lactobacillus in dahi make lactic acid, with no carbon dioxide.

45
00:04:04,433 --> 00:04:10,796
Fermentation itself makes no ATP; the only gain is glycolysis's net two.

46
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:18,343
The respiratory quotient, R Q, is carbon dioxide given out divided by oxygen taken in.

47
00:04:18,343 --> 00:04:20,189
It reveals the fuel being burnt.

48
00:04:20,400 --> 00:04:24,327
For glucose, six over six: R Q is one.

49
00:04:25,533 --> 00:04:30,385
Worked example: show the R Q of the fat tripalmitin is about zero point seven.

50
00:04:30,600 --> 00:04:38,309
Two tripalmitin molecules use one hundred and forty-five oxygen, giving one hundred and two carbon dioxide and ninety-eight water.

51
00:04:38,533 --> 00:04:44,256
Check: carbon one hundred and two each side; hydrogen one hundred and ninety-six each side.

52
00:04:44,466 --> 00:04:51,186
Oxygen: twelve plus two hundred and ninety, three hundred and two, equals two hundred and four plus ninety-eight.

53
00:04:51,186 --> 00:04:51,819
Balanced.

54
00:04:52,033 --> 00:04:57,983
R Q is one hundred and two over one hundred and forty-five: zero point seven zero.

55
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:04,006
Fat is oxygen-poor, so it needs much oxygen: R Q below one.

56
00:05:04,006 --> 00:05:11,220
Malic acid is oxygen-rich: two molecules need six oxygen and give eight carbon dioxide.

57
00:05:11,220 --> 00:05:13,061
R Q one point three three.

58
00:05:13,266 --> 00:05:23,511
So: fat zero point seven, protein about zero point eight, carbohydrate one, malic acid one point three three, oxalic acid four.

59
00:05:25,933 --> 00:05:27,067
Pause and think.

60
00:05:27,067 --> 00:05:30,961
A germinating castor seed burns stored oil.

61
00:05:30,961 --> 00:05:31,564
What R Q?

62
00:05:38,433 --> 00:05:39,984
Zero point seven.

63
00:05:39,984 --> 00:05:45,765
Fats are oxygen-poor, so they use more oxygen than the carbon dioxide they release.

64
00:05:45,765 --> 00:05:48,832
One is the trap: that is for sugar, not oil.

